  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}^{3}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
